WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.040
What’s up, guys? Michael here. I have a
question: When’s the last time you went

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:15.970
to the movies specifically to see a comedy?
What about a rom com? If you’re like most

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:19.260
Americans, it’s probably been a minute.
People in general are skipping out on your

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.470
classic funny-bone-tickling films, and comedies
that succeed at the box office are becoming

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:28.140
the exception rather than the rule. We’re
seeing lots of comedies flounder this year,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.240
from Booksmart to The Beach Bum to The Long
Shot to Late Night and on and on and on. Even

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:39.039
Men in Black: International only pulled in
250 million, a pale comparison to the 624

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:43.249
million its predecessor Men in Black 3 made.&nbsp;
Indeed, you could almost hear the industry

00:00:43.249 --> 00:00:47.740
breathe a collective sigh of relief when the
recent comedy Good Boys hit 21 million in

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.670
its opening weekend, making it one of only
two comedies to hit #1 in the box office this

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.489
year. Comscore media analyst Paul Dergarabedian
summed up the situation, saying, “This is

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:02.639
like a unicorn sighting.” Even so, 21 million
is a sum that probably wouldn’t have even

00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:08.620
made us blink circa the summer of 2009, when
the The Hangover pulled in a cool 45 million

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.050
during its opening weekend.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:14.570
If you scan a list of top performing films
of the late 90s, or even the early to mid

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:18.500
aughts, a whole lot of them are comedies.
What happened to the days when Austin Powers

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.530
ruled the box office? Where’s our 2019 Pretty
Woman? And, just for argument’s sake, why

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.780
no Hangover IV? The truth is traditional film
subgenres like the rom com or those gross

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.250
out comedies of the 1990s seem about as culturally
relevant as a late night sockhop at a high

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.250
school gymnasium.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:38.910
But before you grab that comedy-shaped casket
from your closet, let’s look a little closer.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.090
Is comedy really dead? What if it never even
existed in the first place? Let’s find out

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.240
in this Wisecrack Edition on the maybe death
of comedy?

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.160
We think a quick recap of the current comedy
landscape is in order. News outlets like The

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.120
Hollywood Reporter have made a habit of sounding
the alarm about the death of comedy films.

00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:42.709
We’re treated to ever more dire statistics
showing comedic box office flops,&nbsp; And we’re

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:47.609
seeing fewer laugh out loud comedies Total
box office revenue for comedies shrank from

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:55.030
2.5 billion in 2009 to just 1 billion in 2018.
In 2018, comedies accounted for just 7.2%

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.739
of all domestic box office revenue.

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:02.579
So what made traditional comedy films an endangered
species? You the easy answer might be the

00:03:02.579 --> 00:03:07.709
ever-proliferating streaming services, but
the answer is a little more complicated. Also,

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:09.379
it involves China.

00:03:09.379 --> 00:03:13.389
As you, a presumable human being watching
YouTube, certainly know, consumers have more

00:03:13.389 --> 00:03:17.329
entertainment options than ever before. You
can stream anything your heart desires on

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:21.960
10 different competing platforms or watch
your bestie eat tide pods on YouTube. Note

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.079
that these alternatives are typically cheaper
than&nbsp; a movie ticket.

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:29.359
And how does a studio deal with the competition
from all these sick TikTok compilations? They’re

00:03:29.359 --> 00:03:35.080
going international, or at least, MORE international.
Studios are coping with domestic losses by

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:40.370
trying to maximize their profits in places
like China, Japan, India and France. Indeed,

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:44.510
in 2018, Warner Brothers earned just $1.9
billion dollars in North America, compared

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:50.900
to a hefty 3.6 billion dollars overseas. Across
the board, international audiences are booming,

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.949
and the Motion Picture Association of America
predicts that China will soon overtake the

00:03:54.949 --> 00:03:58.120
US as the largest movie market in the world..

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.170
But how does one make an American film with
widespread international appeal? It’s not

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:11.349
necessarily Meg Ryan really enjoying this
sandwich. “I’ll have what she’s having.”

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:18.140
Instead,&nbsp; it’s usually fast cars, big guns,
and a superhero or two or three… or ten.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:22.500
Indeed, comic book films and action flicks
are a perfect fit for the international audience,

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.000
as they peddle largely in special effects
that can be appreciated regardless of your

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.590
language or culture. As non-dialogue heavy
films, they also require less pesky dubbing

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:35.070
or subtitles, while offering the universal
appeal of watching shit blow up.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:47.900
In contrast, comedies are NOT as easy to port
over to a Bangladeshi theatre screen. That’s

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.750
because comedies rely a lot on cultural specificity.
Just think of how different the British Office

00:04:53.750 --> 00:05:09.400
is from its American counterpart. Or, how
the show also spawned iterations in places

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.790
like India, Chile, and Finland - all with
their own cultural quirks. If you’re still

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.830
thinking comedy can transcend international
borders no problem, peep this 1996 Chinese

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:23.370
comedy God of Cookery. If you’re not from
China - is this funny to you? Probably not.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:27.320
The Rock breaking out of a cast with his bicep
might be universal, but it’s hard to make

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:32.440
a comedy that really appeals to the international
masses. Thus, studios aren’t really investing

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.280
in developing new comedy scripts the way they
used to.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.870
Judd Apatow, the literal creator of the modern
Ford assembly line of comedies, echoes this

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.500
argument, telling Indiewire that comedy film
basically died after the last writer’s strike,

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:49.210
which ended in 2008. Apatow says &nbsp;“I feel
like the studios don’t buy as many scripts

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:53.930
now…. As a result, a lot of great comedy
writers are going to television instead…”

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:58.980
So there you have it, our investigation comes
to a close. Say goodbye to all your favorite

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.450
forms of comedy, from star vehicles for people
like Will Ferrell and Meg Ryan to quality

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:07.390
parodies like Scary Movie to delightful gross
out comedies like There’s Something About

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:10.930
Mary.&nbsp; Instead, if you feel like laughing,
looks like you’re going to have to stick

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:12.560
to TikTok.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:19.560
But wait… what about this? “As far as
I’m concerned, that’s America’s ass”

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:25.670
That line brought the house down. Or take
this moment from Get Out “I’m TS mother***ing

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:31.580
A, we handle sh*t” That’s genuinely funny,
right? Indeed, comedian Jordan Peele’s seamless

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.810
transition into the horror genre hasn’t
stopped him from showcasing his sense of humor.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.440
Even though we’re buying a ticket to see
a horror film, we also walk in pretty sure

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.670
that something’s going to make us laugh.
“By the way I would’ve voted for Obama

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:48.560
for a third term if I could.” Has comedy
just undergone some kind of metamorphosis?

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.330
To understand the cosmic forces at play in
a&nbsp; Marvel movie like Endgame or a horror

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.640
film like Get Out, we’ll need to take a
look at film scholar Geoff King.&nbsp; In his

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:00.710
book Film Comedy, King basically says that
the term “comedy” can’t be fully understood

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:05.530
as just a genre, arguing that it is “so
widespread as to be difficult to locate as

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:11.780
a single or stable generic… form.” Instead,
he suggests looking at comedy as a mode. But...what’s

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:16.990
a mode - and how is it different than a genre?
Simply put, a genre is a category of film.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:21.630
Take, for example, the Western. Nearly all
films within a genre will share certain essential

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:26.120
narratives, characters, or other features,
like setting. Westerns typically have a rugged

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:31.290
cowboy, an expansive dusty American landscape,
an aesthetically unappealing villain and some

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:36.600
form of a gun fight. Both the classic High
Noon and the unclassic Wild Wild West share

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.260
these features and thus, both are westerns.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:45.570
In contrast, a mode is a “manner of presentation.”
It’s a mood or feeling, and all the choices

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:50.000
that contribute to that mood. It’s helpful
here to think of how mode works in music.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.020
A musical mode is a scale with specific notes
and melodic features. Take the Dorian mode,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:03.810
made up of these notes arranged in this manner.
Both the traditional hymn “Drunken Sailor”

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:10.090
and the Latin Jazz songs “Oye Como Va”
are written in the Dorian mode, though the

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:15.500
two songs belong to radically different genres.
Similarly, in film, both Shazam and Once Upon

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:20.790
a Time in Hollywood use a comedic mode, though
the former is also a superhero flick and the

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:26.420
latter is also a thriller and a drama. Got
it? So, King’s argument goes, because “any

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:31.140
genre may be treated as a subject for comedy,'
it's more accurate to see it as a mode, a

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:35.029
modifier of other genres rather than simply
one in its own right.

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:39.650
While Film Comedy was published back in 2002,
we’d argue that King’s point offers a

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:44.530
lens to look at comedy that is even more relevant
today. Fewer comedy movies are made than ever

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:48.890
before, and the ones that are made fail at
the box office. But we might still LAUGH at

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:54.560
the movies just as much. So, King’s diagnosis
of comedy as more of a mode than a genre rings

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.450
truer than ever before. But how exactly does
this “mode” of comedy manifest in movies?

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:06.930
At first glance, that seems simple: Comedy’s
what makes you laugh. But what if, say, in

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:14.080
the cinematic masterpiece The Room, the filmmaker
wasn’t trying to make you laugh? And what

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.580
if a comedy film like Bad Santa doesn’t
make you laugh, even though it’s trying

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:22.791
to? It’s more complicated than just eliciting
a laugh - it needs to be intentional. King

00:09:22.791 --> 00:09:27.490
expands his definition of comedy in film as
a mode that “tends to involve departures

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:32.000
from… what are considered to be the normal
routines of life of the social group in question.”

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.200
Take a normal situation - You’re at the
grocery store. Then&nbsp;add a departure from

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.770
your norm - You’re wearing a bathrobe and
sandals and have to write a check for $0.69

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.150
- Tada! You have comedy.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:46.340
The form of comedy can vary, it can result
from incongruity - like a superhero hanging

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:50.450
out in a convenience store. Or it can result
from exaggeration, like the Dad’s over-the-top

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:56.200
attempt to sound like a tough guy in Us: “So
if y’all wanna get crazy, we can get crazy.”

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:01.320
Ultimately, King says, comedy results “from
a sense of things being out of place, mixed

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:02.940
up or not quite right.”

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:08.010
Furthermore, modality isn’t just in the
tone of the words being spoken or images being

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:13.120
shown. King notes that modality can be “complex
and multi-dimensional”, and include elements

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.880
like the use of music, sound, or opening sequences.
For instance, we can see how music is used

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:22.840
when Alison Williams’s character hilariously
drinks a glass of milk segregated from a bowl

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:30.710
of fruit loops while listening to the song
"(I've Had) The Time of My Life.” Both sound

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:35.190
and even the use of color are funny in context.
The cheery tune calls attention to the incongruity

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:39.820
of a diabolical character drinking a wholesome
glass of milk while literally looking for

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:44.420
the next mark for her parent’s weirdo gang
of brain-swapping yuppies.&nbsp; At the same time,

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:49.650
the flat-out bizarre method of cereal consumption
defies common sense. Seriously - she doesn’t

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:54.360
even eat an entire fruit loop in one bite.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:58.780
Or let’s look at how characters, narrative
and tone can combine to create a comedic mode.

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:03.110
Take the opening scenes of last year’s Halloween
reboot, in which a couple of true crime podcasters

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:07.500
attempt to interview Michael Myers. The whole
sequence is full of self-aware commentary

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:12.260
on true crime culture and the commodification
of popular criminal personalities. “We’re…

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.860
we’re making a podcast.” “We’re investigative
journalists!” This scene feels decidedly

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.140
more comedic than anything we saw in the film’s
1978 predecessor. You can see the modality

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:21.730
of comedy working its way in, even as the
film prepares us for lots of bloodshed.

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:27.529
And, of course, we can’t talk about modern
comedy without dealing with Marvel, which

00:11:27.529 --> 00:11:32.020
has basically turned the superhero film into
a debatably knee-slapping comedic experience

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:53.310
with silly one liners, absurdist situations,
and all the self-aware humor you could want.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:12:01.270
Now one caveat - We think it’s reasonable
to assume that, in simpler times of filmmaking,

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:04.990
it would have made more sense to view comedy
as a genre. That’s because before the late

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:10.160
1950s, films typically conformed pretty neatly
into defined categories, and were less likely

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:14.490
to borrow from one another. It was unlikely
that you would see a sci-fi - drama like Arrival

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:22.600
or a funny drama like Juno. As a result, comedy
may have acted more like a consistent genre

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:33.290
and less like a mode. But today, we’re seeing
genres becoming increasingly more unstable.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:43.930
Indeed, films today are more likely to take
a buffet style approach to genre. Oftentimes,

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:48.300
these are used to appeal to more media-savvy
audiences who have come to recognize tropes

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:52.770
in a more conscious way. As this happens,
we see comedy becoming almost like ambient

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:54.149
background music.

00:12:54.149 --> 00:12:58.850
Once we start thinking of comedy as mode rather
than a genre, it becomes clear that our comic

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:04.170
sensibilities are not only alive, but perhaps
even thriving. With the world an ever-larger

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:08.060
dumpster fire, maybe we’re more in need
of a laugh than ever - even if we’re not

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:12.980
seeking it in the form of a washed up newscaster
and his accidental profanity “Go f**k yourself

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:14.149
San Diego”

00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:18.660
And of course, if you’re looking for a laugh,
just turn on the TV, where comedy is more

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:22.160
than alive and well in the form of It’s
Always Sunny in Philadelphia, South Park,

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:27.190
Rick and Morty,&nbsp; Bojack Horseman, Silicon
Valley and on and on and on.&nbsp; Even still,

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:32.180
we’d say comedy is also surviving on the
big screen as a mode, and if you agree with

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:35.029
King, that’s all it ever was in the first
place.

00:13:35.029 --> 00:13:39.560
So what do you guys think? Is comedy in film
deader than Uncle Ben after another Spiderman

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:49.520
reboot, or totally alive and well in unexpected
ways? Are you digging Jordan Peele’s brave

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:53.930
new world of horror comedies or do you miss
the days of Tom Cruise running a brothel in

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:58.420
Risky Business?? Let us know in the comments.
And a big thanks to all our dope patrons who

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.100
support the channel and our podcasts. Don’t
forget to hit that subscribe button -

